    "content": "## The Asymmetry of Executive Contracts\n\nWhen corporate boards extend an executive employment offer, the conversation naturally centers on base salary, target bonuses, and long-term equity grants. However, the most critical clauses governing financial security are those that dictate what happens when strategy shifts, boards change, or acquisitions occur.\n\nExecutive severance and retention agreements are not hostile or pessimistic; they are standard risk management instruments designed to ensure executives make bold corporate decisions without fearing personal financial catastrophe.\n\n## The Five Essential Levers in Executive Agreements\n\n1. **Double-Trigger Change of Control**: Ensure that if the company is acquired (Trigger 1) and your role is diminished or terminated within 12 to 24 months (Trigger 2), 100% of unvested equity and performance stock units (PSUs) vest immediately.\n2. **Pro-Rata Bonus Guarantees**: Standard employment agreements forfeit annual bonuses if you depart prior to the payout date. Executive agreements must stipulate that bonuses are paid pro-rata based on elapsed months and target performance.\n3. **Salary & Benefits Continuation**: Standard executive benchmarks mandate 12 to 24 months of base salary continuation alongside continuous health and dental coverage.\n4. **Garden Leave vs. Unpaid Non-Competes**: If the board insists on a 6-to-12-month non-compete covenant, mandate full base salary compensation throughout the restricted duration.\n5. **Legal & Outplacement Allowances**: Standard packages include $15,000 to $25,000 in dedicated legal fee reimbursements for your independent counsel during contract execution.\n\n## Navigating Departures with Professional Dignity\n\nWhen an executive transition occurs, mutual non-disparagement, clean release of claims, and an agreed-upon joint press release protect both your industry reputation and the company's market capitalization. Always negotiate a structured communication timeline that enables you to brief your team before public disclosure."
